Review the following C++17 code using Google C++ style, prioritizing correctness. Give concrete findings and a minimal correction. The public signature must remain exactly `void Schedule(std::string id, int& count)`; do not prohibit `int&`, change that interface, or upgrade the toolchain. `Enqueue` runs the callback later, potentially after `Schedule` returns, on a different thread. The caller guarantees `count` remains alive until all callbacks finish and does not access it concurrently with callback execution; callbacks themselves are serialized.

```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void Enqueue(std::function<void()> task);

void Schedule(std::string id, int& count) {
  Enqueue([&]() {
    std::cout << id << '\n';
    ++count;
  });
}
```

